    "created_at": "2019-05-14T15:05:13Z",
    "updated_at": "2019-05-14T15:05:13Z",
    "body": "Some may in some circumstances, but in general it's not something that's easy to do except by the author. Duplicate links may have different but equivalent urls, same url but different link attributes like `download`, different link text (\"Article title\" vs. \"Read more about Article Title\"), or a mix of the above.\r\n\r\nThey could keep a set of seen links defined over the tuples `(link text, link url, relevant link attributes)` and only announce when they hit a link-tuple not in that set. That would get all the obvious duplicates and would work on my page but it also requires that the link text be the same which gigs you in a lot of a11y testing tools that complain about duplicate link text for this very reason. I'd guess that anything more advanced than that would amount to some heuristics atop that, like removing \"Read more about \" from the link text before testing, which can help with common patterns but are otherwise easily foiled.\r\n\r\nAnother AT that might benefit are the various extensions that let you follow any link in the viewport by typing a short prefix code, like https://github.com/philc/vimium/wiki/Using-Link-Hints (I'm not sure why I didn't think of that earlier since that's something I personally use regularly.) It would still need to use some heuristics, unless the duplicate said which link it is a duplicate of requiring ids which are gross, as the canonical link may not be in the viewport. Knowing that a link is a duplicate of something might help the search, though. At the very least, it could de-prioritize duplicate link so that non-duplicate links get shorter codes.",
